  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n- A couple rounds of showers/storms today\n\nMUCAPE values early this Wednesday morning are a very respectable\n1,500 to 3,000 j/kg. Upstream convection continues to fill in, in\nan east/west zone across Wisconsin. A weaker low level jet around\n30 knots is driving this convection. We expect it to continue to\nslide east and/or develop over our CWA from I-96 to the north.\nCannot rule out severe weather this morning given the\naforementioned instability and deep layer shear of 40 knots. Both\nhail and stronger wind gusts are possible. Some high resolution\nmodels indicate a few swaths of severe wind. We will be monitoring\nas the activity continues to build in. \n\nGiven westerly upper flow that will result in training of cells\nas well as a persistent influx of high PWAT air from the south, \nheavy rain is certainly possible. \n\nAnother round of showers and storms is possible this afternoon and\nevening and SPC has much of our forecast in a Slight Risk for\nsevere weather. MUCAPE values will recover to in excess of 2,000\nj/kg with deep layer shear on the order of 30 knots. Machine\nlearning schemes favor area off to our east into the Thumb more\nthan our area, but additional severe weather cannot be ruled out.\nSARS sounding analogs via the HRRR show a fairly big signal for\nlarge hail. DCAPE values are in excess of 1,000 j/kg so severe\ngusts are also possible. Bottom line today could be an active day\n(both this morning and later this afternoon) with severe weather\nand heavy rain. \n\nA Heat Advisory has been issued for the I-94 corridor. This is the\narea in the CWA that has the best chance at hitting 100 apparent\ntemperatures. It is valid from noon to 8pm this evening.\n\n- Additional showers/storms on Thursday\n\nLow level moisture remains parked over the area on Thursday with a\nsurface front lingering. Additional rounds of showers and storms\nappear likely on Thursday although convergence of the low level \njet does not look ideal. The low level jet weakens through the \nday. Shortwave activity will be working through the area however \nin zonal upper flow north of the ridge located in the Southeast \nU.S. The upper support combined with low level convergence \nassociated with the front should be enough to drive additional \nactivity. Details remain difficult to nail down however as \nconvection today and tonight will affect the setup for tomorrow. \nWe are in a SPC marginal for severe weather on Thursday. \n\n- Storm chances could linger into Friday/Saturday\n\nGFS and ECWMF ensemble data show quite a few members that support\nmore showers and storms Friday and potentially into Saturday. The\nupper flow shifts to the northwest as a ridge develops in the\nplains states. There are indications that convective vorts will be\ndriving southeast into our area during this period. So, the bottom\nline is we will see additional activity. With repeated rounds of\nshowers and storms we will need to keep an eye on the threat of \nlocally heavy rain.",
